fter looking across the manuscript ecosystem, scholarly infrastructure, current AI labs, preservation standards, agent protocols, and the current Pāṭala repo, I think the strategy becomes much more precise.

The winning Pāṭala is not the place that owns every manuscript, model, OCR system, viewer, paper index, renderer, or peer-review workflow.

It is the layer that makes all of them interoperable around one question:

    What exactly exists, what has actually been observed about it, what is currently justified, who says so, what remains unknown, and what interaction with reality would change that state?

That is what almost nobody else is building end-to-end.

And importantly, you're slightly past “we need an API.” The current repo already reports a 22-table Atlas/resolver/API and ~43 routes, but only about 254 resolved works against ~32k SOURCE objects. So the immediate bottleneck is coverage + canonical resolution + interoperability, not writing another backend.
