4. Muktabodha + IFP are especially strategically valuable

Muktabodha reports more than 3,000 preserved texts and more than 570 searchable e-texts, with particularly strong holdings across Kashmir Śaivism, Śaiva traditions, Śrīvidyā, Śākta and related corpora; more than 380 searchable texts were edited from manuscripts under Mark Dyczkowski's supervision.

The French Institute of Pondicherry describes an enormous manuscript collection with especially deep Śaiva-siddhānta holdings.

Those are much more strategically interesting to you than indiscriminately ingesting another million Bhagavad-Gītā-derived pages.

I would eventually approach organizations like these with:

    “Keep your archive. Keep your rights. Keep your identifiers. We want to connect your resources into the live research graph, expose demand for your materials, send scholarly corrections back, and potentially route funded requests toward holdings whose digitization would resolve actual research problems.”

That is a credible institutional offer.
